  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/ddb1bce0c22f83b4333c4552081cb527dde48da8dfafddb392c1cc3db12c702d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(3x)+3?$",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(3x)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/fc63b6078542e010a6203b139e59c82132e08038e713a295a39377c137f5fbaa.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(3x)+3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/5ee1df4a6b8624f25d4de8915d38fe12eb591bd08ed183879d8e94d4721e3a76.png)",
